  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "change" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "A change that adds a generated artifact goes red in CI on a job that is not a required lane, reporting HealAuthorCommitRequired against a path under .github/workflows that the author never opened, with the cause ActionsJobCredentialScopeUnavailable." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "The emitted workflow carries a staging line per committed generated artifact, derived from the registry, so registering an artifact CHANGES the workflow. The repairing job would ordinarily regenerate and push that itself, and for workflow files it cannot: the credential an Actions job runs under is not permitted to modify workflows. So it refuses, names the path, and requires the author to carry the emission. Observed on the change that introduced this very page -- one staging line for docs/onboarding.md -- so the mechanism is this row's own receipt rather than a report." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: DocumentedRemedy { remedy: "Run the whole-registry regenerator the refusal names, then commit the workflow it rewrites. THE CAUSE STRING DOES NOT SAY THIS AND IT IS THE WHOLE POINT: a credential-scope refusal on a workflow path is not a defect in your change and not something a retry clears -- it is a step the repairing job is structurally unable to take for you. Confirm the regenerator rewrote ONLY paths you can explain: on this change it left every other generated artifact byte-identical, which is what distinguishes carrying your own emission from importing somebody else's drift." as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "run-witnesses" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "The floor refuses in namespace-wave admission because origin/main cannot be resolved, after a run that was otherwise clean for twenty-odd minutes. Nothing earlier in the path asked for origin/main and nothing said it would be needed." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "Wave admission compares the working tree against the base branch, so it resolves origin/main; a clone that does not carry that ref cannot answer and the lane refuses at the end rather than the start. WHO THIS ACTUALLY BITES, STATED NARROWLY BECAUSE THE WIDE VERSION IS WRONG AND WOULD MISLEAD THE ONE READER IT IS FOR: the clone this page prescribes is a FULL clone and a full clone HAS origin/main, so a first-time contributor following the clone step literally does not hit this. It bites anyone working from a shallow, filtered or single-branch clone -- which is every reviewer checking out a PR branch, and was every walker who had to reach a head that is not on main. The defect the page owns is therefore not a missing fetch in the clone step: it is that origin/main is first NEEDED here and first MENTIONED at the landing step, and that gap is real at any clone depth." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: DocumentedRemedy { remedy: "Fetch the base ref before running the lane rather than after the refusal: a clone made with --depth, --filter or --single-branch needs origin/main explicitly, and the refusal costs a full lane run to discover. If you cloned the way the clone step says, you already have it and this row is not your problem -- read it as a note for the day you check out somebody else's branch. The landing step names origin/main again for the merge check; that is the same ref and not a second obligation." as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "run-witnesses" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "The lane runs for twenty-five minutes and then refuses with wet-refusal claims, and the binaries the reader built by hand are all present. Building every declared bin, which the row above recommends, does not help." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "Some witnesses build a temporary crate of their own DURING the run, and that nested cargo inherits the same environment the reader had to escape at the build step -- so it goes through the wrapper, dies in the compiler cache, and leaves the binary it was producing missing. The failure then surfaces as a claim refusal rather than as a build error. THIS IS A DIFFERENT CAUSE FROM THE MISSING-BIN ROW ABOVE AND ITS REMEDY DOES NOT REACH IT: the reader's own binaries are fine, and no amount of building them fixes a build that happens inside the run." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: DocumentedRemedy { remedy: "THIS REMEDY IS CONFIRMED BY AN INDEPENDENT WALK AND WAS CARRIED AS UNTESTED UNTIL ONE RAN. It was written from a walker's reading of what they observed, and they said plainly that they had not re-run the twenty-five-minute lane to confirm it; the page carried that label rather than promote a plausible remedy to an established one. A later walker, working from a fresh clone with no contact with this page's author, applied it and the lane completed: the wet work finished every claim it planned with no refusals, and the floor came back clean with executed equal to planned. Re-derive that by running the lane, not by trusting this sentence. WHAT TO DO: export the same escape the build step needed -- the compiler wrapper cleared, and the toolchain's own cargo ahead of any wrapper on the path -- FOR THE PROCESS THAT RUNS THE LANE, rather than applying it only to the build-step command line, because the builds the lane spawns inherit its environment and they are the ones failing. If the refusals name wet or self-host receipts while your own binaries are present, suspect the nested build before the corpus. This is the missing-binary arm of the three-way classification above, reached by a route the reader cannot see." as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "run-witnesses" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "Scoping the run to one failing witness produces a FAIL that is not about the witness: it reports a hermetic route gap, saying no arm exists for some operation and that the claim never reached its subject." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "claim_batch IS HERMETIC BY DEFAULT, and the witnesses that fail at this step are WET -- they shell out, build a crate and run the binary they built. A wet claim invoked without the wet flag therefore never reaches its subject at all, and the refusal you get back is about the missing route rather than about the claim. IT NAMES ITSELF CORRECTLY: it says the claim never reached its subject, so this is a route gap and not a verdict. gunbc.explicit_witness_admission known_red_class_note carries the measurement, on a named witness, of exactly this shape -- a wet row invoked by the hermetic recipe returning FAIL while naming the dispatches it could not route." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: DocumentedRemedy { remedy: "SCOPE THE RUN, DO NOT RE-RUN THE LANE. An earlier revision of this page told you the opposite -- that a wet witness runs in the lane and nowhere else, so isolating one was not available and the twenty-five minute lane was the only instrument. THAT WAS WRONG, it was the single most expensive sentence on this page, and a walker spent a twenty-five minute lane run obeying it. The recipe belongs to gunbc.explicit_witness_admission known_red_class_note and is stated there rather than restated here: claim_batch takes an entry and a function list, AND FOR AN ExecutionWitnessKind ROW THE WET FLAG AS WELL, without which the recipe cannot do what it says. Read the flag list off that authority, because it owns them and this page does not. WHY THE FLAGLESS FORM MISLEADS, which is the whole reason this row exists: without the flag the scoped run still FAILS, and it fails in a way that looks like a verdict about your witness. It is not. It is the hermetic default refusing to route an effect, so a reader who takes it at face value concludes that scoping does not work and goes back to the lane -- which is precisely the wrong conclusion this page drew once already." as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "propose" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "A clone over an anonymous transport can fetch and build everything, and cannot push." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "Read access is anonymous and write access is not. Nothing in the path up to this point requires an account, so the first credential requirement appears at the moment the work is finished." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: OperatorGated { who_must_act: "whoever administers repository access; provisioning is a separate item and is deliberately not specified here" as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: true,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "land" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "The forge reports the pull request as mergeable while a real conflict against the base exists." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "The forge's mergeability field has been observed reporting clean over conflicting trees, so it is not an oracle for whether a merge applies. Reported by a sibling lane in this subtree and carried here because the step it breaks is the last one." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: DocumentedRemedy { remedy: "Establish mergeability locally by writing a merge tree for the head against the base and reading its conflict report, rather than from the forge's summary field." as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
  OnboardingObstacle {
    step_id: "land" as NonEmptyStr,
    symptom: "The change is reviewed, green and mergeable, and nothing happens." as NonEmptyStr,
    cause: "A review is advisory: it establishes that no blocking defect was found, never that the change is what was asked for. The merge itself is performed under a standing policy by the operator, so the last step of the path is the one step that structurally is not self-serve." as NonEmptyStr,
    disposition: OperatorGated { who_must_act: "the operator, who performs the merge; this is the single irreducible operator dependency remaining in the path and it is a policy choice rather than a gap" as NonEmptyStr },
    discoverable_from_symptom: false,
  },
]

// WHAT A FIRST-DAY READER ACTUALLY NEEDS FROM DESIGN.md, which the item asks for by name.
// The ordering is not a summary of DESIGN.md and must not become one -- a second, shorter account
// of the axioms would be exactly the nicknaming fork the document forbids. Each row names a
// section and says WHY a reader needs it at that point, so the reader reads the authority itself.
data onboarding_design_reading: List<DesignReading> = [
  DesignReading {
    section: "Building and checks" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: FirstDay,
    why: "It names the checks and the one manual per-clone seed, and it is the section this path is linked from. It is the only section a reader needs before a build." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 1, the objective" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: FirstDay,
    why: "Three axioms and the three consequences every later section is derived from. Without it the rest of the document reads as a list of preferences rather than a chain, and a reader will argue with conclusions instead of premises." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 5, fail-closed" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: BeforeFirstCommit,
    why: "It carries the hard reject: a silent widen, a fabricated default, an uncounted degradation or an escape hatch is refused regardless of what else the change delivers. It is the single most likely reason a first diff is rejected." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Sections 2 and 3, redundancy and single authority" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: BeforeFirstCommit,
    why: "They decide where a fact is allowed to live. A first change usually either restates something that already has an owner or cites a position instead of a symbol, and both are refused on these two sections." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Sections 3b and 3c, conformance and consumption" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: BeforeFirstCommit,
    why: "They are the two questions a reviewer asks of a diff: does it inhabit the models it touches, and who consumes what it adds. A well-shaped declaration nothing reads is refused under the second." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 4b, the guarantee ladder" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: BeforeFirstCommit,
    why: "It fixes what a change may claim about its own safety, and it is where the vocabulary a reviewer will use comes from. A claim stated one rung above its executed evidence is the failure mode it names." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 4, the substrate, and section 4c, annotations" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: WhenItBites,
    why: "Needed at the first .dag edit rather than at the first read: the closed vocabulary the language is built from, and the rule that a comment is the only place uncarried prose may live." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 6, how to work" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: WhenItBites,
    why: "The authorship tells -- scaffold, workaround, for-now, a new artifact with no consumer. Read it when a change starts wanting one of those, which is the moment it is about to be refused." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
  DesignReading {
    section: "Section 7, self-hosting, and the recurring failure modes" as NonEmptyStr,
    priority: WhenItBites,
    why: "Why Rust is present at all and what the seed is shrinking toward, plus the ledger of classes already found. Deferrable on day one and indispensable once a change touches the compiler." as NonEmptyStr,
  },
]

fn blocker_rendered(b: OnboardingBlocker) -> String {
  match b {
    SelfServe => "self-serve"
    NeedsCredential { credential } => concat("needs a credential: ", (credential as String))
    NeedsOperatorDecision { decision } => concat("needs an operator decision: ", (decision as String))
    NeedsMachine { resource } => concat("needs a machine: ", (resource as String))
  }
}

fn blocker_is_self_serve(b: OnboardingBlocker) -> Bool {
  match b {
    SelfServe => true
    NeedsCredential { credential: _ } => false
    NeedsOperatorDecision { decision: _ } => false
    NeedsMachine { resource: _ } => false
  }
}

fn disposition_rendered(d: ObstacleDisposition) -> String {
  match d {
    RemediedByDerivation { how } => concat("REMEDIED BY DERIVATION. ", (how as String))
    DocumentedRemedy { remedy } => concat("DOCUMENTED REMEDY. ", (remedy as String))
    OperatorGated { who_must_act } => concat("OPERATOR GATED. ", (who_must_act as String))
  }
}

fn disposition_is_operator_gated(d: ObstacleDisposition) -> Bool {
  match d {
    RemediedByDerivation { how: _ } => false
    DocumentedRemedy { remedy: _ } => false
    OperatorGated { who_must_act: _ } => true
  }
}

fn reading_priority_is_first_day(pr: DesignReadingPriority) -> Bool {
  match pr {
    FirstDay => true
    BeforeFirstCommit => false
    WhenItBites => false
  }
}

// A READING ORDER WITH NOTHING MARKED FIRST-DAY RESTATES THE PROBLEM INSTEAD OF ANSWERING IT
// (review 64627). The item's obstacle is that DESIGN.md is not a first-day document, so a roster
// of only BeforeFirstCommit and WhenItBites rows leaves a reader with nothing to read before
// their first build -- and a count-greater-than-zero check stays green over exactly that roster.
// The predicate takes the list so the discriminating input can be authored.
fn readings_have_a_first_day(readings: List<DesignReading>) -> Bool {
  fold(readings, init: false, f: fn(acc, r) { acc || reading_priority_is_first_day(pr: r.priority) })
}

fn first_day_readings() -> List<DesignReading> {
  filter(onboarding_design_reading, r => reading_priority_is_first_day(pr: r.priority))
}

fn priority_rendered(pr: DesignReadingPriority) -> String {
  match pr {
    FirstDay => "first day"
    BeforeFirstCommit => "before the first commit"
    WhenItBites => "when it bites"
  }
}

fn steps_blocked_on_others() -> List<OnboardingStep> {
  filter(onboarding_steps, s => !blocker_is_self_serve(b: s.blocker))
}

fn operator_gated_obstacles() -> List<OnboardingObstacle> {
  filter(onboarding_obstacles, o => disposition_is_operator_gated(d: o.disposition))
}

fn obstacles_undiscoverable_from_symptom() -> List<OnboardingObstacle> {
  filter(onboarding_obstacles, o => !o.discoverable_from_symptom)
}

fn step_ids() -> List<String> {
  map(onboarding_steps, s => (s.id as String))
}

// THE MEMBERSHIP TEST TAKES THE POPULATION RATHER THAN REBUILDING IT (review 65441).
//
// step_id_is_declared used to call step_ids() itself, so a fold over obstacles rebuilt the whole
// id list and walked it once per obstacle -- O(obstacles x steps) with a fresh allocation each
// time. DESIGN section 2's repair for demands sharing an ancestor is to carry the first value, and
// section 6 says a proven cost shape is fixed regardless of the realised n. So the list is resolved
// ONCE at the fold below and handed in.
//
// step_id_is_declared survives as the single-id query, because a caller asking about one id has no
// population to share and the enrolled discriminating arm asks exactly that.
fn id_is_declared_in(ids: List<String>, id: String) -> Bool {
  fold(ids, init: false, f: fn(acc, declared) { acc || declared == id })
}

fn step_id_is_declared(id: String) -> Bool {
  id_is_declared_in(ids: step_ids(), id: id)
}

// EVERY OBSTACLE NAMES A DECLARED STEP. An identity join, not a count: an obstacle attached to a
// step that does not exist is unreachable from the path a reader walks, so it is invisible
// exactly when it fires.
fn obstacles_with_undeclared_step() -> List<OnboardingObstacle> {
  let declared = step_ids()
  filter(onboarding_obstacles, o => !id_is_declared_in(ids: declared, id: (o.step_id as String)))
}

// WHY THERE IS NO GAPLESS-ORDINAL CHECK, AND NO ORDINAL (review 65422).
//
// Each step used to carry an authored `ordinal`, and a fold asserted the set was a gapless total
// order -- by filtering the whole list once per step, which is the quadratic shape DESIGN section 6
// says is always fixed regardless of the realised n. But the quadratic scan was the SYMPTOM. The
// defect was that the order of a list was authored a second time inside its own elements, so the
// two could disagree and a validator was needed to notice.
//
// The position now comes from the list. A duplicate or a gap has no constructor, so the check is
// not made linear -- it is DELETED along with the field and the two fixtures that fed it, which is
// DESIGN section 5's preference for making the state unwritable over catching it. This is the same
// repair the rule roster above received, and the step ordinal was the last authored position left.

fn step_blocks(s: OnboardingStep, position: Int) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  let head = h3(text: join([integer_int_to_decimal_string(value: position), ". ", (s.id as String)], ""))
  let goal = p(text: (s.goal as String))
  let who = p(text: join(["Who is needed: ", blocker_rendered(b: s.blocker)], ""))
  let verify = p(text: join(["Done when: ", (s.verification as String)], ""))
  match s.command {
    Absent => [head, goal, who, verify]
    Present { value } => [head, goal, p(text: join(["`", (value as String), "`"], "")), who, verify]
  }
}

fn obstacle_item_text(o: OnboardingObstacle) -> String {
  join(
    [
      "**", (o.step_id as String), " — ", (o.symptom as String), "** ",
      (o.cause as String), " ",
      disposition_rendered(d: o.disposition), " ",
      if o.discoverable_from_symptom {
        "The error text points at the remedy."
      } else {
        "THE ERROR TEXT DOES NOT POINT AT THE REMEDY, which is why this row exists."
      },
    ],
    ""
  )
}

fn reading_item_text(r: DesignReading) -> String {
  join(["**", (r.section as String), " — ", priority_rendered(pr: r.priority), ".** ", (r.why as String)], "")
}


fn onboarding_preamble_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h1(text: "gunbc — from a bare clone to one landed change"),
    p(text: "Generated from `gunbc.contributor_onboarding_path`. Do not hand-edit — the generated-artifact phase adjudicates this file against that authority. Every command below is derived from the authority that owns it, so this page moves when the build, the lane partition or the source roots move."),
    p(text: "This page is an index of a path, not the proof that it works. What establishes the path is walking it: clone, build, run the lanes, make one change, get it reviewed, land it. If a step here does not work, that is a defect in the path and worth reporting as one — the obstacle roster below exists because each of its rows was hit rather than imagined."),
    p(text: "`DESIGN.md` is the authority for every judgement about a change and is not a first-day document. The reading order at the end of this page says what you need before your first build, what you need before your first commit, and what can wait."),
    p(text: join(["**", integer_int_to_decimal_string(value: count(onboarding_rules)), " RULES APPLY TO EVERY STEP BELOW, because three strangers walking this page lost their build to them.**"], "")),
    ol(items: map(onboarding_rules, r => li(text: join(["**", (r.name as String), ".** ", (r.rule as String)], "")))),
    hr(),
  ]
}

// THE ONE WALK THAT KNOWS A STEP'S POSITION, because the list is now the only place the order
// lives. The accumulator carries the next position alongside the blocks emitted so far, so a step
// is numbered by WHERE IT IS rather than by what it claims about itself, and inserting a step
// renumbers everything after it with no edit anywhere.
type StepRenderWalk {
  position: Int
  blocks: List<MarkdownBlock>
}

fn rendered_step_blocks(steps: List<OnboardingStep>) -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  fold(
    steps,
    init: StepRenderWalk { position: 1, blocks: [] },
    f: fn(acc, s) {
      StepRenderWalk {
        position: acc.position + 1,
        blocks: concat(acc.blocks, step_blocks(s: s, position: acc.position)),
      }
    }
  ).blocks
}

fn onboarding_step_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  flat_map(
    [
      [
        h2(text: "The path"),
        p(text: join([integer_int_to_decimal_string(value: count(onboarding_steps)), " steps in order. Run each verification before moving on: a step that half-worked is the expensive kind, because the failure surfaces several steps later attached to the wrong cause."], "")),
      ],
      rendered_step_blocks(steps: onboarding_steps),
    ],
    bs => bs
  )
}

fn onboarding_obstacle_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "What bites, and where"),
    p(text: "Each row is attached to the step it breaks. The last sentence of each row says whether the error text explains itself, because that is the difference between a newcomer losing minutes and a newcomer needing someone who already knows."),
    ul(items: map(onboarding_obstacles, o => li(text: obstacle_item_text(o: o)))),
  ]
}

fn onboarding_bus_factor_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Where the path still needs somebody else"),
    p(text: "Stated as a census rather than as reassurance. These are the steps and obstacles a document cannot discharge, and they are the standing bus-factor risks in this path."),
    ul(items: map(steps_blocked_on_others(), s => li(text: join([(s.id as String), ": ", blocker_rendered(b: s.blocker)], "")))),
    p(text: "Obstacles whose only honest disposition is that somebody else must act:"),
    ul(items: map(operator_gated_obstacles(), o => li(text: join([(o.step_id as String), ": ", disposition_rendered(d: o.disposition)], "")))),
  ]
}

fn onboarding_reading_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "Reading DESIGN.md in an order"),
    p(text: "`DESIGN.md` is reasoned serially from three axioms, and every section is a consequence of the ones before it. So it rewards being read in its own order and punishes being skimmed for the section that seems relevant. What follows is when you need each part, not a summary of any of them — a shorter second account of the axioms would be the exact duplication the document forbids."),
    ul(items: map(onboarding_design_reading, r => li(text: reading_item_text(r: r)))),
  ]
}

fn onboarding_checks_blocks() -> List<MarkdownBlock> {
  [
    h2(text: "The other commands worth knowing"),
    p(text: "Derived from `gunbc.repo_self_build`, which is the single point through which this repository builds and checks itself."),
    ol(items: [
      li(text: join(["Lint over every target, which a merge is blocked on: `", repo_self_clippy_command(), "`"], "")),
      li(text: join(["The package's own unit tests, which are local diligence and are run by no required step: `", repo_self_test_command(), "`"], "")),
      li(text: join(["The source roots every entry is resolved against: `", join(source_root_flags(), " "), "`"], "")),
    ]),
  ]
}

fn onboarding_document() -> MarkdownDocument {
  MarkdownDocument {
    blocks: flat_map(
      [
        onboarding_preamble_blocks(),
        onboarding_step_blocks(),
        onboarding_obstacle_blocks(),
        onboarding_bus_factor_blocks(),
        onboarding_checks_blocks(),
        onboarding_reading_blocks(),
      ],
      bs => bs
    )
  }
}

fn expected_contributor_onboarding_md() -> String {
  serialize_markdown_source(doc: onboarding_document())
}
